  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 311 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nA much quieter afternoon and evening is anticipated across the\narea following the stabilizing influences of this morning's\nround of showers and thunderstorms. Accordingly, we have\ncontinued the trend of lowering POPs through tonight, with dry\nconditions expected across most of the area. Our main concern\nfor tonight is the threat for fog development, as a very moist\nlow-level airmass nocturnally cools amidst light winds. Also \ncannot rule out the possibility for some of the fog to become \nlocally dense into early Thursday morning, particularly in areas\nthat become mostly clear. \n\nOn Thursday, forecast attention will be focused on the \nevolution of the next MCS expected to track eastward from the \nPlains out across the Corn Belt tonight. Ultimately, this MCS is\nexpected to begin to cross the Mississippi River around, or a \nbit after daybreak on Thursday, though likely in a weakening \nstate as the primary instability gradient over IL looks to \nremain south of I-80 Thursday morning. This may thus act to \nsteer the stronger storm potential with the MCS farther south \nacross central parts of IL later in the morning. In spite of \nthis, high precipitable water values (~1.9\") and deep warm cloud\ndepths will support locally heavy rainfall with this remnant \nMCS. \n\nLater Thursday, and especially Thursday night, a south- \nsouthwesterly low-level jet is expected to ramp up across the \nmid- Mississippi Valley and nose into IA and partitions of \nnorthern IL. Interestingly, this could result in regenerating \nshowers and thunderstorms along a west-northwest to east-\nsoutheast corridor in the wake of Thursday MCS. Where this \noccurs, a flash flood threat would be a big concern Thursday \nnight, especially given the storm motion to favor training cells\nover the same areas. At this time, there are signs that the \nbest potential for several hours of training storms may remain \nsouthwest of the Chicago metro area, and potentially even \nsouthwest of our area altogether, but this will need to be \nmonitored. \n\nUnfortunately, medium-range guidance continues to depict our\nactive pattern remaining in place and supporting continued waves\nof showers and thunderstorms across our region into the weekend.\nFortunately, there does appear to be some decent agreement in\nthe global models/ensembles that high pressure eventually\nsettles across the western Lakes region by Monday-Tuesday of\nnext week. Once this occurs, it will push our stalled frontal\nzone to the south and provide us with a much needed break in\nthis active weather pattern.\n\nKJB",
